"use strict";(self.webpackChunkcasdoor_website=self.webpackChunkcasdoor_website||[]).push([["7358"],{91895(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"casdoor-v4","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/casdoor-v4","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/blog/2026-10-30-casdoor-v4.mdx","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-30-casdoor-v4.mdx","title":"Casdoor v4: A New Console, a Marketplace, and OAuth for MCP Servers","description":"Casdoor v4.0 came out on September 1. Two months and 25 releases later we are at v4.25, so this is a good point to look back at what changed. In short: the admin console was rebuilt, self-hosting got a lot less fiddly, there is now a Marketplace for integrations and sign-in themes, and Casdoor can act as the authorization server for MCP servers and AI agents.","date":"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Release","permalink":"/blog/tags/release"},{"inline":true,"label":"IAM","permalink":"/blog/tags/iam"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"OAuth","permalink":"/blog/tags/o-auth"},{"inline":true,"label":"MCP","permalink":"/blog/tags/mcp"},{"inline":true,"label":"Self-hosting","permalink":"/blog/tags/self-hosting"}],"readingTime":4.83,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"casdoor-v4","title":"Casdoor v4: A New Console, a Marketplace, and OAuth for MCP Servers","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","Release","IAM","SSO","OAuth","MCP","Self-hosting"],"date":"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"Running Casdoor on Kubernetes: Official Helm Chart and Configuration as Code","permalink":"/blog/casdoor-on-kubernetes"}},"content":"Casdoor v4.0 came out on September 1. Two months and 25 releases later we are at v4.25, so this is a good point to look back at what changed. In short: the admin console was rebuilt, self-hosting got a lot less fiddly, there is now a Marketplace for integrations and sign-in themes, and Casdoor can act as the authorization server for MCP servers and AI agents.\\n\\nIf you haven\'t used it: Casdoor is an open-source identity and access management platform written in Go. It is a full identity provider, not a library or a login proxy. Users live in Casdoor, Casdoor issues the tokens, and your apps talk to it over OAuth 2.0, OpenID Connect, SAML 2.0, CAS, LDAP or SCIM. Authorization is handled by [Apache Casbin](https://casbin.apache.org), so ACL, RBAC and ABAC models all work. It is Apache-2.0 licensed and listed in the CNCF Landscape.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## A rebuilt console and sign-in pages\\n\\nv4.0 replaced the Ant Design admin console with a new one built on shadcn/ui, Tailwind and Vite (now on React 19). It has a command palette, bulk row selection, inline form validation, two-column forms and tables where you can hide columns. Switching languages no longer reloads the page.\\n\\nThe sign-in, sign-up and password reset pages were redesigned too. Dark mode works properly, and the pages are usable on a phone.\\n\\nIf you are upgrading from v3, read the upgrade guide linked from the README first. The main thing to check is the path of the frontend build if you serve static files through a CDN or reverse proxy.\\n\\n## Easier to self-host\\n\\nA lot of the v4 work came from issues and forum threads by people running Casdoor themselves:\\n\\n- **No default password.** On first visit Casdoor asks you to set the `admin` password, or you can set it with `initAdminPassword`.\\n- **Sub-path deployment.** Set `basePath` to serve Casdoor at `https://example.com/auth/` instead of its own host.\\n- **Forward auth.** Casdoor has a forward auth endpoint for Traefik, Caddy and Nginx, so you can put apps that have no login of their own behind it, with public paths and per-site rules.\\n- **Docker tags.** Images are now also tagged `X`, `X.Y` and `X.Y.Z`, so you can follow a major or minor version.\\n- **Helm.** The chart moved into the main repository, is published to GHCR and [Artifact Hub](https://artifacthub.io/packages/helm/casdoor/casdoor) with every release, and can hold organizations, applications and users as configuration. See [Running Casdoor on Kubernetes](/blog/casdoor-on-kubernetes).\\n- **Windows service.** The binary can run as a Windows service.\\n- **Upgrades.** The system info page shows the latest release and can upgrade an official binary in one click.\\n- **Operations.** Container health checks, Prometheus counters for sign-in success and failure, audit logs forwarded to Syslog, and Redis Cluster support.\\n\\n## Marketplace\\n\\nThe new [Marketplace](https://marketplace.casdoor.com) lets an admin install integrations from inside Casdoor: application templates, identity providers and sign-in themes. Installing one fills in the configuration for you, and uninstalling restores what was there before.\\n\\nThere are more than 20 sign-in themes so far. You can preview a theme on your own sign-in page before installing it. Organization admins can also customize the sign-in pages with their own fonts, images and HTML, within an allowlist that blocks forms and scripts. The read-only demo at [door.casdoor.com](https://door.casdoor.com) cycles through the themes if you want to see them.\\n\\n## OAuth for MCP servers and AI agents\\n\\nThe MCP specification asks remote MCP servers to use OAuth, and MCP clients usually don\'t know in advance which authorization server they will meet. Casdoor now covers the pieces this needs:\\n\\n- OAuth 2.1 authorization code flow with PKCE, and a consent page where users can grant only some of the requested scopes\\n- Dynamic Client Registration (RFC 7591) and client management (RFC 7592)\\n- Client ID Metadata Documents: an MCP client can use a URL as its `client_id` without registering first\\n- `private_key_jwt` client authentication for dynamically registered clients\\n- Resource Indicators (RFC 8707) and Protected Resource Metadata (RFC 9728)\\n- Token Exchange (RFC 8693), including ID-JAGs for enterprise-managed authorization, so an organization\'s IdP can decide which agents may reach which MCP servers\\n\\nPoint your MCP server\'s protected resource metadata at Casdoor, and sign-in, consent, token issuing and validation are handled for you. Casdoor also runs its own MCP server, so an assistant such as Claude Code or Cursor can manage users, applications and permissions through it.\\n\\n## Other protocol work\\n\\n- SAML Single Logout and IdP-initiated SSO\\n- OIDC `prompt=none`, better conformance for hybrid flow, `prompt` and `max_age`, and the RFC 9207 `iss` parameter\\n- Token revocation (RFC 7009), DPoP and the device authorization flow\\n- Magic link sign-in, password history checks, and passkeys shared across sibling subdomains\\n- Exclusive sign-in with a maximum number of sessions per user\\n\\n## Try it\\n\\nThe quickest way to look around:\\n\\n```shell\\ndocker run -p 8000:8000 casbin/casdoor-all-in-one\\n```\\n\\nThen open http://localhost:8000. The image includes SQLite and sample data, so use it for trying things out, not for production. The [installation guide](/docs/basic/server-installation) covers real deployments.\\n\\nThere are two online demos: [demo.casdoor.com](https://demo.casdoor.com) is writable and resets every five minutes, and [door.casdoor.com](https://door.casdoor.com) is read-only.\\n\\nIf you would rather not run it yourself, [Casdoor Cloud](https://www.casdoor.com/pricing) gives you a dedicated, managed instance at a flat price with no per-user fees. New accounts get $10 in credit to try it.\\n\\n## Links\\n\\n- GitHub: https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor\\n- Release notes: https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/releases\\n- Docs: https://casdoor.ai/docs/overview\\n- Discord: https://discord.gg/5rPsrAzK7S\\n\\nBug reports and feature requests are welcome on GitHub. If Casdoor is useful to you, a star helps other people find it."},{"id":"casdoor-on-kubernetes","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/casdoor-on-kubernetes","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/blog/2026-10-07-casdoor-on-kubernetes.mdx","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-07-casdoor-on-kubernetes.mdx","title":"Running Casdoor on Kubernetes: Official Helm Chart and Configuration as Code","description":"Casdoor has an official Helm chart. It lives in the main repository under manifests/casdoor, is published with every Casdoor release to GHCR, and is listed on Artifact Hub. Starting with Casdoor v4.18.0, the chart can also hold your organizations, applications, users and providers as declarative configuration: change them in the values file, run helm upgrade, and the running pods apply the change without a restart.","date":"2026-10-07T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Kubernetes","permalink":"/blog/tags/kubernetes"},{"inline":true,"label":"Helm","permalink":"/blog/tags/helm"},{"inline":true,"label":"GitOps","permalink":"/blog/tags/git-ops"},{"inline":true,"label":"Configuration as Code","permalink":"/blog/tags/configuration-as-code"},{"inline":true,"label":"IAM","permalink":"/blog/tags/iam"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/blog/tags/sso"}],"readingTime":5.59,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"casdoor-on-kubernetes","title":"Running Casdoor on Kubernetes: Official Helm Chart and Configuration as Code","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","Kubernetes","Helm","GitOps","Configuration as Code","IAM","SSO"],"date":"2026-10-07T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Casdoor v4: A New Console, a Marketplace, and OAuth for MCP Servers","permalink":"/blog/casdoor-v4"},"nextItem":{"title":"Casdoor Joins CNCF Cloud Native Landscape","permalink":"/blog/casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape"}},"content":"Casdoor has an official Helm chart. It lives in the main repository under [`manifests/casdoor`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/tree/master/manifests/casdoor), is published with every Casdoor release to GHCR, and is listed on [Artifact Hub](https://artifacthub.io/packages/helm/casdoor/casdoor). Starting with Casdoor v4.18.0, the chart can also hold your organizations, applications, users and providers as declarative configuration: change them in the values file, run `helm upgrade`, and the running pods apply the change without a restart.\\n\\nThis post walks through a Kubernetes deployment from the first `helm install` to a setup you can keep in Git.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Install\\n\\nYou need Helm v3.8+ and a cluster:\\n\\n```shell\\nhelm install casdoor oci://ghcr.io/casdoor/helm-charts/casdoor\\n```\\n\\nThe chart version is the Casdoor version, so `--version 4.18.0` installs Casdoor 4.18.0. Without an Ingress or Gateway, the service is only reachable inside the cluster. To try it, forward a port:\\n\\n```shell\\nkubectl port-forward svc/casdoor 8000:8000\\n```\\n\\nThen open `http://localhost:8000`. On the first visit, Casdoor asks you to set the password of `built-in/admin`; then sign in with it. See [Sign in to the admin console](/docs/basic/server-installation#sign-in).\\n\\n## A production setup\\n\\nThe defaults are meant for a quick try: Casdoor stores its data in SQLite inside the container, so it is lost when the pod is replaced. For a real deployment, put the settings below in a `values.yaml` file and install with `helm install casdoor oci://ghcr.io/casdoor/helm-charts/casdoor -f values.yaml`.\\n\\n### External database\\n\\n```yaml\\ndatabase:\\n  driver: postgres # or mysql, cockroachdb\\n  host: postgres.db.svc.cluster.local\\n  user: casdoor\\n  password: change-me\\n  databaseName: casdoor\\n  sslMode: require\\n```\\n\\nTo keep the database password out of the values file, put the whole `app.conf` in a Secret and set `configFromSecret` to its name.\\n\\n### Exposing Casdoor\\n\\nThe chart supports both a classic Ingress and the Gateway API. With an Ingress controller and cert-manager:\\n\\n```yaml\\ningress:\\n  enabled: true\\n  className: nginx\\n  annotations:\\n    cert-manager.io/cluster-issuer: letsencrypt-prod\\n  hosts:\\n    - host: door.example.com\\n      paths:\\n        - path: /\\n          pathType: Prefix\\n  tls:\\n    - secretName: casdoor-tls\\n      hosts:\\n        - door.example.com\\n```\\n\\nWith the Gateway API (Istio, Envoy Gateway, Cilium, Kong, NGINX Gateway Fabric and others), attach an `HTTPRoute` to an existing Gateway:\\n\\n```yaml\\ngatewayApi:\\n  enabled: true\\n  parentRefs:\\n    - name: my-gateway\\n      namespace: gateway-system\\n  hostnames:\\n    - door.example.com\\n```\\n\\nThe chart can also create the Gateway itself and add an HTTP to HTTPS redirect, see the [Helm guide](/docs/basic/try-with-helm#exposing-casdoor).\\n\\n### More than one replica\\n\\nCasdoor keeps sign-in sessions on local disk by default. Before running more than one replica, point all of them to the same Redis, so a user stays signed in whichever pod serves the request. Every `app.conf` setting can be passed as an environment variable, so the endpoint can come from a Secret:\\n\\n```yaml\\nreplicaCount: 3\\n\\nenvFromSecret:\\n  - name: redisEndpoint\\n    secretName: casdoor-redis\\n    key: endpoint # host:port, or host:port,db,password\\n```\\n\\nThe same Redis is used for the OAuth device flow state. Once Redis is in place, `autoscaling.enabled: true` adds a HorizontalPodAutoscaler.\\n\\n## Configuration as code\\n\\nClicking organizations and applications together in the web UI is fine for a first test, but most teams want the setup of their identity provider reviewed and versioned like the rest of their infrastructure. The `initData` values do that:\\n\\n```yaml\\ninitData:\\n  enabled: true\\n  data:\\n    organizations:\\n      - owner: admin\\n        name: acme\\n        displayName: Acme\\n        passwordType: bcrypt\\n    applications:\\n      - owner: admin\\n        name: app-acme-portal\\n        organization: acme\\n        displayName: Acme Portal\\n        redirectUris:\\n          - https://portal.acme.example.com/callback\\n    users:\\n      - owner: acme\\n        name: alice\\n        displayName: Alice\\n        password: change-me\\n        signupApplication: app-acme-portal\\n    roles:\\n      - owner: acme\\n        name: admins\\n        displayName: Admins\\n        users: [acme/alice]\\n        isEnabled: true\\n```\\n\\nThe chart stores the data in a Secret and mounts it into the pods. Casdoor applies it at startup, then checks it every 30 seconds (`initData.watchInterval`). When you change the data and run `helm upgrade`, kubelet updates the mounted Secret and the running pods apply it without a restart.\\n\\nA few rules make this safe to use alongside the web UI:\\n\\n- **Only the fields you write are managed.** An object that already exists gets the fields set in the file; its other fields keep the values edited in the UI. You can manage just the redirect URIs of an application, and leave the rest to the admin console.\\n- **Passwords are initial passwords.** A user\'s `password` is only used when the user is created, so users can change theirs afterwards.\\n- **Nothing is deleted.** Removing an object from the file doesn\'t remove it from Casdoor.\\n- **Errors don\'t take Casdoor down.** If an object fails to apply, the error is logged and Casdoor keeps serving; the data is retried on every check until it applies.\\n\\nThe file format is the one Casdoor has always used for [data initialization](/docs/deployment/data-initialization): organizations, applications, users, providers, certs, roles, permissions, groups, LDAP servers, webhooks, plans, pricings and more, with the same field names as the REST API. A quick way to get started is to export an existing instance with `casdoor -export` and keep the objects you want to manage.\\n\\nTo keep the data out of the values file, for example when it is managed by Sealed Secrets or External Secrets, create the Secret yourself:\\n\\n```yaml\\ninitData:\\n  enabled: true\\n  existingSecret: casdoor-init-data\\n  existingSecretKey: init_data.yaml\\n```\\n\\nThe same feature works without Kubernetes. Mount a JSON or YAML file and set:\\n\\n```ini\\ninitDataFile = /init-data/init_data.yaml\\ninitDataMerge = true\\ninitDataWatchInterval = 30\\n```\\n\\n## Or use Terraform\\n\\nIf you prefer `terraform plan`, imports of existing objects and drift detection, the official [Terraform provider](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/casdoor/casdoor) manages the same objects through the Casdoor API:\\n\\n```terraform\\nresource \\"casdoor_application\\" \\"portal\\" {\\n  name          = \\"app-acme-portal\\"\\n  organization  = \\"acme\\"\\n  redirect_uris = [\\"https://portal.acme.example.com/callback\\"]\\n}\\n```\\n\\nUse the Helm values when the configuration should ship with the deployment, and Terraform when Casdoor is one of many systems you already manage with it. See the [Terraform guide](/docs/deployment/terraform).\\n\\n## Coming from the old chart\\n\\nUp to v4.15.0, the chart was published as `oci://registry-1.docker.io/casbin/casdoor-helm-charts`, and the separate `casdoor/casdoor-helm` repository is now archived. The Docker Hub chart still receives every release, but new installs should use the GHCR one. Moving an existing release over is a regular upgrade, and resource names stay the same:\\n\\n```shell\\nhelm upgrade casdoor oci://ghcr.io/casdoor/helm-charts/casdoor --version <version>\\n```\\n\\n## Links\\n\\n- Helm chart: [Artifact Hub](https://artifacthub.io/packages/helm/casdoor/casdoor) \xb7 [source](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/tree/master/manifests/casdoor)\\n- Guides: [Try with Helm](/docs/basic/try-with-helm) \xb7 [Data initialization and configuration as code](/docs/deployment/data-initialization#configuration-as-code) \xb7 [Terraform](/docs/deployment/terraform)\\n- Don\'t want to run it yourself? [Casdoor Cloud](https://www.casdoor.com/pricing?utm_source=casdoor.ai&utm_medium=blog&utm_content=kubernetes) gives you a dedicated Casdoor instance that we host and keep upgraded for you."},{"id":"casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/blog/2026-02-22-casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape.mdx","source":"@site/blog/2026-02-22-casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape.mdx","title":"Casdoor Joins CNCF Cloud Native Landscape","description":"On February 22, 2026, Casdoor joined the CNCF Cloud Native Landscape, under the Security & Compliance category in Provisioning. The Landscape brings together widely used cloud-native projects and products; inclusion helps users and enterprises discover and evaluate solutions for building cloud-native systems.","date":"2026-02-22T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"CNCF","permalink":"/blog/tags/cncf"},{"inline":true,"label":"Cloud Native","permalink":"/blog/tags/cloud-native"},{"inline":true,"label":"Landscape","permalink":"/blog/tags/landscape"},{"inline":true,"label":"IAM","permalink":"/blog/tags/iam"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"Security","permalink":"/blog/tags/security"}],"readingTime":1.94,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape","title":"Casdoor Joins CNCF Cloud Native Landscape","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","CNCF","Cloud Native","Landscape","IAM","SSO","Security"],"date":"2026-02-22T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Running Casdoor on Kubernetes: Official Helm Chart and Configuration as Code","permalink":"/blog/casdoor-on-kubernetes"},"nextItem":{"title":"Enhancing App Security and User Experience with Casdoor Face Authentication SSO","permalink":"/blog/face-authentication-casdoor-sso"}},"content":"On February 22, 2026, **Casdoor** joined the [CNCF Cloud Native Landscape](https://landscape.cncf.io/), under the **Security & Compliance** category in Provisioning. The Landscape brings together widely used cloud-native projects and products; inclusion helps users and enterprises discover and evaluate solutions for building cloud-native systems.\\n\\n[https://landscape.cncf.io/?item=provisioning--security-compliance--casdoor](https://landscape.cncf.io/?item=provisioning--security-compliance--casdoor)\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n<img src=\\"/img/blog/cncf-landscape-casdoor.png\\" alt=\\"Casdoor on CNCF Landscape - Security and Compliance section\\" style={{maxWidth: \'100%\', height: \'auto\', border: \'1px solid #eee\', borderRadius: \'8px\'}} />\\n\\n*Casdoor on the CNCF Cloud Native Landscape (Security & Compliance)*\\n\\n## About Casdoor\\n\\n<div style={{display: \'flex\', alignItems: \'center\', gap: \'1rem\', flexWrap: \'wrap\', marginBottom: \'1rem\'}}>\\n\\n<img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/casdoor-logo_1185x256.png\\" alt=\\"Casdoor\\" style={{height: \'80px\', width: \'auto\'}} />\\n\\n</div>\\n\\nCasdoor is an open-source, UI-first Identity and Access Management (IAM) / Single Sign-On (SSO) platform with a web UI. It supports OAuth 2.0, OIDC, SAML, and CAS, as well as many social logins (Google, GitHub, WeChat, etc.), and helps you build user management and SSO in a few steps. Casdoor is developed by [Casbin](https://casbin.org) in Golang and React. It uses a front-end/back-end separation architecture, supports high concurrency, and is actively maintained by the community. The project has received over 13k GitHub stars.\\n\\n## About CNCF and the Cloud Native Landscape\\n\\n<div style={{display: \'flex\', alignItems: \'center\', gap: \'1.5rem\', flexWrap: \'wrap\', marginBottom: \'1rem\'}}>\\n\\n<img src=\\"https://www.cncf.io/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/cncf-main-site-logo.svg\\" alt=\\"CNCF - Cloud Native Computing Foundation\\" style={{height: \'80px\', width: \'auto\'}} />\\n<img src=\\"https://landscape.cncf.io/images/logo_header.svg\\" alt=\\"CNCF Cloud Native Landscape\\" style={{height: \'80px\', width: \'auto\'}} />\\n\\n</div>\\n\\nThe **Cloud Native Computing Foundation (CNCF)** is a Linux Foundation project that promotes cloud-native technologies. Its vision is to make cloud native ubiquitous. CNCF maintains the **Cloud Native Landscape** ([https://landscape.cncf.io](https://landscape.cncf.io)), which classifies and tracks projects and products in the cloud-native ecosystem and serves as a reference map for developers and enterprises.\\n\\n## Looking ahead\\n\\nJoining the CNCF Landscape reflects progress in Casdoor\u2019s maturity and community. We will keep improving Casdoor and its integration with cloud-native workflows, and we welcome more users and contributors to join the community.\\n\\nRelated links:\\n\\n- [Casdoor](https://casdoor.org) \xb7 [GitHub](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor) \xb7 [Documentation](https://casdoor.org/docs) \xb7 [CNCF Landscape \u2013 Casdoor](https://landscape.cncf.io/?selected=casdoor)"},{"id":"face-authentication-casdoor-sso","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/face-authentication-casdoor-sso","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/blog/2023-12-12-face-authentication-casdoor-sso.mdx","source":"@site/blog/2023-12-12-face-authentication-casdoor-sso.mdx","title":"Enhancing App Security and User Experience with Casdoor Face Authentication SSO","description":"In an era where digital identity management is crucial, Single Sign-On (SSO) solutions like Casdoor are revolutionizing the way users interact with multiple applications. Casdoor\'s SSO simplifies the login process by allowing users to authenticate just once to access multiple services. This not only enhances user convenience but also boosts engagement and satisfaction.","date":"2023-12-12T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"Single-Sign-On","permalink":"/blog/tags/single-sign-on"},{"inline":true,"label":"Face Recogniztion","permalink":"/blog/tags/face-recogniztion"},{"inline":true,"label":"FaceID","permalink":"/blog/tags/face-id"},{"inline":true,"label":"Face","permalink":"/blog/tags/face"},{"inline":true,"label":"Machine Learning","permalink":"/blog/tags/machine-learning"}],"readingTime":1.61,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"James Lawson","title":"Product Manager","url":"https://casdoor.org","imageURL":"https://cdn.casbin.com/casdoor/static/admin.png","key":"James Lawson","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"face-authentication-casdoor-sso","title":"Enhancing App Security and User Experience with Casdoor Face Authentication SSO","authors":["James Lawson"],"tags":["Casdoor","SSO","Single-Sign-On","Face Recogniztion","FaceID","Face","Machine Learning"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Casdoor Joins CNCF Cloud Native Landscape","permalink":"/blog/casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape"},"nextItem":{"title":"Welcome","permalink":"/blog/welcome"}},"content":"In an era where digital identity management is crucial, Single Sign-On (SSO) solutions like Casdoor are revolutionizing the way users interact with multiple applications. Casdoor\'s SSO simplifies the login process by allowing users to authenticate just once to access multiple services. This not only enhances user convenience but also boosts engagement and satisfaction.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nFor businesses, Casdoor\'s SSO streamlines user credential management, enhancing security and simplifying new user onboarding. It counters the risk associated with weak or recycled passwords by centralizing user management.\\n\\nHowever, in the face of growing online threats, integrating robust security measures in SSO systems like Casdoor is vital. This is where face authentication technology comes into play, adding an extra layer of protection while maintaining user-friendly interactions.\\n\\nFace authentication employs unique facial characteristics to confirm user identities. It compares live images with stored facial templates, offering a secure yet straightforward way to authenticate users. This method boasts high accuracy, even under varied conditions, and is widely adopted in modern consumer devices.\\n\\nIntegrating face authentication into Casdoor\'s SSO system promises a secure, efficient, and seamless user experience. To ensure optimal performance, it is crucial to focus on:\\n\\n1. *Anti-Spoofing Measures*: Implementing liveness detection, like tracking eye movements or random gestures, is essential to counteract spoofing attempts and maintain system integrity.\\n\\n2. *Regular Updates for Improved Accuracy*: Keeping the face authentication technology updated with the latest AI and biometric advancements ensures accuracy and enhances user experience. It\'s vital to use diverse datasets for algorithm training to prevent biases.\\n\\n3. *Balancing Security and Convenience*: The best systems strike a perfect balance between stringent security measures and user convenience. This approach encourages user adoption by ensuring a straightforward authentication process without compromising security.\\n\\nIn conclusion, by integrating face authentication with Casdoor\'s SSO, businesses can offer a secure, efficient, and user-friendly login experience. This not only fortifies security but also aligns with modern user expectations in the digital world."},{"id":"welcome","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/welcome","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/blog/2021-08-24-welcome.mdx","source":"@site/blog/2021-08-24-welcome.mdx","title":"Welcome","description":"Welcome to Casdoor official website!\u{1F44B}","date":"2021-08-24T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"Authentication","permalink":"/blog/tags/authentication"},{"inline":true,"label":"OAuth2.0","permalink":"/blog/tags/o-auth-2-0"},{"inline":true,"label":"Casbin","permalink":"/blog/tags/casbin"}],"readingTime":1.43,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"ErikQQY","title":"Casbin Member","url":"https://github.com/ErikQQY","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/52615090?s=120&v=4","key":"ErikQQY","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"welcome","title":"Welcome","authors":["ErikQQY"],"tags":["Casdoor","SSO","Authentication","OAuth2.0","Casbin"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Enhancing App Security and User Experience with Casdoor Face Authentication SSO","permalink":"/blog/face-authentication-casdoor-sso"}},"content":"Welcome to Casdoor official website!\u{1F44B}\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What is Casdoor?\\n\\nCasdoor is a UI-first [Identity Access Management (IAM)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Identity_management) / [Single-Sign-On (SSO)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Single_sign-on) platform based on OAuth 2.0, OIDC, SAML and CAS.\\n\\nCasdoor is developed by [Casbin](https://casbin.org) using Golang and React.\\n\\nCasdoor aims at providing a powerful authentication platform to handle the user management.\\n\\n## Why use Casdoor?\\n\\n* \u26A1\uFE0F Powerful feature and simple config. With support for  <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_google.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_github.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_facebook.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_twitter.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_linkedin.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_weibo.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_wechat.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_qq.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_dingtalk.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img>   <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_gitee.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img>  <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_wecom.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img><img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_gitlab.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> sign in and traditional username/password sign in, Casdoor helps you implement your users management system just in a few steps.\\n\\n* \u{1F4A5} Front-end back-end separation architecture. Casdoor provides maneuverable web UI and supporting high concurrency.\\n\\n* \u{1F942} Active open source community. Casdoor is open source and actively maintained.\\n\\n* \u{1F310} Multi-language support. Casdoor supports multiple languages, including Chinese, English, French, and many more.\\n\\n\\n## How to use Casdoor?\\n\\nYou can follow the detailed document to learn about [Casdoor API](https://door.casdoor.com/swagger/) and [setup your own Casdoor](/docs/basic/server-installation). Or use the [Casdoor SDKs](/docs/how-to-connect/sdk) to implement your own application."}]}}')}}]);